@section 24.6 | Age Structure and Demographic Dividend

@q 16:167-175

@table
Age fact (2011) | Figure
Children 0-14 | **About 29-31%** (30.8% final)
Under 35 | About **65%**
Old-age dependency ratio | **14.2%**
Median age | About **29 years** (2020)
**Demographic dividend** | Growth gain when the **working-age (15-59/64)** share is high; India's window runs to about **2055**
Dependent population | **0-14** and **60+**
Dependency ratio falling | Because the **15-59** share is rising
@end

@q 16:176-178

@q 16:185-186

The **National Literacy Mission** (1988) aimed at **75% literacy by 2007**. The progressive population-resource regions include the **Ganga-Yamuna Doab** and **Tamil Nadu**. A growing **working population** is a gain, not a harm, of population growth; about **2%** of India's people had a disability in 2011.

@up UP link: UP's sex ratio is **912** and child sex ratio **902** (2011); **Jaunpur** (1,024) has the highest and **Gautam Buddha Nagar** (851) the lowest district ratio. UP has the **most SCs** (4.14 crore) and **most Muslims** in India, but the **lowest ST share** (0.6%). Its TFR fell from 2.7 (NFHS-4) to **2.4 (NFHS-5)**.

